{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "# Native Sparse Attention (DeepSeek NSA) Notebook\n\n> Hands-on Build It and Exercises."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Build It"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "`code/main.py` implements the three branches on a short synthetic sequence and shows:"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- The compression MLP (a simple mean-pool baseline is used for pedagogical clarity; the real NSA uses a learned MLP)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- The top-k block selection driven by compressed-branch scores."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- The sliding-window attention on the last `w` tokens."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- The gated combination."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "- A compute-count printout comparing to full attention."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 1: compress tokens into blocks"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```python"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "def compress(K, l):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    n = len(K)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    n_blocks = (n + l - 1) // l"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    out = []"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    for b in range(n_blocks):"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        start, end = b * l, min((b + 1) * l, n)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        block = K[start:end]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        summary = [sum(row[d] for row in block) / len(block) for d in range(len(K[0]))]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "        out.append(summary)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "    return out"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "```"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 2: compressed-branch attention"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Run softmax attention of the query against the compressed keys. The compressed-branch scores double as the signal for top-k selection."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 3: top-k block selection"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Pick the indices of the `k` highest-scoring compressed blocks. Load the original uncompressed tokens from those blocks and run attention on them."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 4: sliding-window attention"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Take the last `w` tokens and run standard attention against them."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 5: gate + combine"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "A small MLP on the query produces three gate weights. The final output is a weighted sum of the three branch outputs."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "### Step 6: compute counting"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "Print the number of keys attended per query for each branch and the total. Compare to `N` (full attention). On a 1024-token synthetic with `l = 32, k = 4, w = 128`, NSA sees `32 + 128 + 128 = 288` keys per query versus 1024 for full attention \u2014 3.5x fewer."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "## Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": [
        "1. Run `code/main.py` on a 1024-token synthetic. Sweep `(l, k, w)` across three presets and print compute counts. Identify the preset that achieves the lowest key-count per query while keeping 95% recall against full attention on a needle-in-haystack test.\n\n2. Replace the mean-pool compressor with a tiny learned MLP (2-layer, hidden 32). Train it on a synthetic task where the signal is the average of a block. Measure the perplexity gap against the mean-pool baseline on held-out data.\n\n3. Implement the gate MLP. It takes the query as input and outputs three scalars. Show that the gate behaves sensibly: near-uniform weighting on random queries, heavy weight on the selected branch when the query hits a far-back block.\n\n4. Compute the KV cache memory budget for an NSA-enabled 70B model at 128k context. KV heads are 8, head dim 128, BF16. Compare to full attention and to MLA (Phase 10 \u00b7 14 showed MLA's numbers). Identify the sequence length where NSA's fine-grained branch KV cache equals full attention.\n\n5. Read Section 4 of the NSA paper (arXiv:2502.11089) and explain in three sentences why the compressed branch's attention scores are reused for top-k selection rather than computing a separate routing score. Tie the answer to gradient flow."
      ]
    }
  ],
